t{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 3+(-11)\\cdot (-4)+17\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 5+(-11)\\cdot 4+17\\cdot 1}{{5}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-81601/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acb4bd03-9b89-5775-a700-e1f3ea9fa62e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81601"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81594/selection/891019872246ebd6aba6993193196e1a40ebd05db3830d5797144fb315f4b02f" :answer/id #uuid "0e7ddbd2-8f20-56d4-8279-1e1d1a66a46c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81594/selection/4e7645fa00e49d3e14b3c072cb19b32f3f0d679cc9901941506965fd31dd6d94" :answer/id #uuid "017b9a99-a383-5aef-bbd1-4912ca325cab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81594/selection/f94d6280cbf4bc5e611138ab8efe3a675289d02387d3a639cce39fc4a9ffbd57" :answer/id #uuid "132d1bd0-ba63-5d24-af55-336ee1a315c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81594/selection/bfea6b3f513b11bef39e38e35847f2ab9e05ce4cdc49db959d6252cab9e9c0e8" :answer/id #uuid "6b19f978-61fa-5143-8b8f-7ab351545a47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -13\\end{aligned}]." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81594/selection/ddd61c507f1f1ce9219d8ffee65f108e92d324031824977e754858b0b088ae77" :answer/id #uuid "599d5004-fffc-57ef-8e9b-0a2350dfc27e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81594/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a4f15928-7232-5a5a-a699-dfe2df51438c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-81594/selection/f94d6280cbf4bc5e611138ab8efe3a675289d02387d3a639cce39fc4a9ffbd57" :answer-field/choices ["q-81594/selection/891019872246ebd6aba6993193196e1a40ebd05db3830d5797144fb315f4b02f" "q-81594/selection/4e7645fa00e49d3e14b3c072cb19b32f3f0d679cc9901941506965fd31dd6d94" "q-81594/selection/f94d6280cbf4bc5e611138ab8efe3a675289d02387d3a639cce39fc4a9ffbd57" "q-81594/selection/bfea6b3f513b11bef39e38e35847f2ab9e05ce4cdc49db959d6252cab9e9c0e8" "q-81594/selection/ddd61c507f1f1ce9219d8ffee65f108e92d324031824977e754858b0b088ae77"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81594" :question/id #uuid "4faefb01-3257-5fa8-9e41-d2b284e76cae" :question/math-academy-id "q-81594" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{15\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 6+(-3)\\cdot (-12)}{{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-12)^{2}}v=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{15\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=4[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-81594/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acb4bd03-9b89-5775-a700-e1f3ea9fa62e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81594"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140367/selection/73eeaf3fae024b54005d89a5185040ad67cf4b6c72d401d6ad32bf0e79c13de9" :answer/id #uuid "cdb6c99c-76a4-5925-9618-c8976a394310" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140367/selection/faad0727b2c62b6cced88abf9b210ea8763c2fd1c03c01c27346d66484992037" :answer/id #uuid "41dc1eec-6d8a-5357-8005-13576ffef99a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140367/selection/9f6bfd6fa85d96298a99db901d9b73f609ef64ac0bcf5fbdc4a7ac6d3b733558" :answer/id #uuid "6cdc04cf-3499-5f62-99f7-1da7b8ed286f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140367/selection/05402db46107d70ece210f0bd5e24b332ae4f43bd474d592035c3e3c92625629" :answer/id #uuid "22c1d0d4-da84-52bf-b93e-0c13066a06df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140367/selection/f00d8b7a5eeed0dcaa02b7f16c350647a90a461726a760f9ecdf0379dbf8fa63" :answer/id #uuid "72912aa4-533e-502d-9af4-6deae7cce76f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140367/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d52d91bf-103b-5e2c-b620-5b7047927f8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140367/selection/faad0727b2c62b6cced88abf9b210ea8763c2fd1c03c01c27346d66484992037" :answer-field/choices ["q-140367/selection/73eeaf3fae024b54005d89a5185040ad67cf4b6c72d401d6ad32bf0e79c13de9" "q-140367/selection/faad0727b2c62b6cced88abf9b210ea8763c2fd1c03c01c27346d66484992037" "q-140367/selection/9f6bfd6fa85d96298a99db901d9b73f609ef64ac0bcf5fbdc4a7ac6d3b733558" "q-140367/selection/05402db46107d70ece210f0bd5e24b332ae4f43bd474d592035c3e3c92625629" "q-140367/selection/f00d8b7a5eeed0dcaa02b7f16c350647a90a461726a760f9ecdf0379dbf8fa63"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140367" :question/id #uuid "835e6f56-4b55-54bc-8dba-c3d154485354" :question/math-academy-id "q-140367" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{6\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{6\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(6-4)}^{2}+{(-1-3)}^{2}+{(-4-(-2))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140367/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140367"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-82917/selection/6cf1ba8fcecf7ff74fcb48e7f39a2cabed6551908e76b1f229ada74cff9b7d1c" :answer/id #uuid "0dc9f3ea-1462-51df-9f38-5426df413617" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82917/selection/7346ebe1e5d722acf040262ae8e6e53d26ca80a32da08982328df7eef98b7372" :answer/id #uuid "f5653dfe-4837-5ff5-9133-bcd1a008b353" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82917/selection/a038cf4df09c65d711c1174200281cc22b8be446497e9dc942f4f18d8fa119e2" :answer/id #uuid "10af7bc7-932b-5785-a44a-2e121b933894" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82917/selection/38a654cf90258344795cdc55f520d917120f8918da5a14b69b639b6c97b4a41c" :answer/id #uuid "6c3d900e-729e-5dec-bbd0-737c2b9aa3ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82917/selection/bb5e3e524771fed0f8298cd585cdb5759ed07bb1ac0ac034d00b73a0b668a9ce" :answer/id #uuid "73d7fe21-207a-581c-a616-dfec4041ddf4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82917/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "941b7fe8-b35d-57e7-9b93-d8aa8a066cc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-82917/selection/38a654cf90258344795cdc55f520d917120f8918da5a14b69b639b6c97b4a41c" :answer-field/choices ["q-82917/selection/6cf1ba8fcecf7ff74fcb48e7f39a2cabed6551908e76b1f229ada74cff9b7d1c" "q-82917/selection/7346ebe1e5d722acf040262ae8e6e53d26ca80a32da08982328df7eef98b7372" "q-82917/selection/a038cf4df09c65d711c1174200281cc22b8be446497e9dc942f4f18d8fa119e2" "q-82917/selection/38a654cf90258344795cdc55f520d917120f8918da5a14b69b639b6c97b4a41c" "q-82917/selection/bb5e3e524771fed0f8298cd585cdb5759ed07bb1ac0ac034d00b73a0b668a9ce"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-82917" :question/id #uuid "d55179c8-8008-5c36-8302-ab011fa8de1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-82917" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}},$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 4+(-9)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}], \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot 0+(-9)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 1}{(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+(-9)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 4}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}}{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-0)^{2}+(-3-(-5))^{2}+(-9-(-2))^{2}+(3-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{63} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-82917/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8eb07a32-45bd-5975-9f61-de6294d4895d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-82917"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-40591/selection/d62732e72e17b0393290bd9e548ef368f6b7424bded817d038b07db5bf5fcb98" :answer/id #uuid "f89a3ec3-9b17-5ea4-aad5-da0f9531eb5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40591/selection/986e06f062dcc0ad37fbb185f7e0d56b0fd8e74b571035e7dc0af3d06af89951" :answer/id #uuid "7ad88ab1-0e8e-5c9f-b5b5-93b706ad4cdc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40591/selection/82f0679237d603c9b88d77fbc6f240065a24a3716631fab4d9f58426a7f011e5" :answer/id #uuid "adbc3792-e676-5444-99d0-f82f1796a199" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40591/selection/7b1b8538b264eef6681d0ab5f3b14d51b6cdff67f34fba59d0886ae5c6acf80a" :answer/id #uuid "456ea0a6-32b5-5da5-9939-2aeac5f40db5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40591/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "f56efbf8-5a4e-5254-841c-79e27aadb84d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40591/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "64aaf035-1ccf-532e-aa60-a9577abd171a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-40591/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-40591/selection/d62732e72e17b0393290bd9e548ef368f6b7424bded817d038b07db5bf5fcb98" "q-40591/selection/986e06f062dcc0ad37fbb185f7e0d56b0fd8e74b571035e7dc0af3d06af89951" "q-40591/selection/82f0679237d603c9b88d77fbc6f240065a24a3716631fab4d9f58426a7f011e5" "q-40591/selection/7b1b8538b264eef6681d0ab5f3b14d51b6cdff67f34fba59d0886ae5c6acf80a" "q-40591/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-40591" :question/id #uuid "bac6bfb5-a42f-5950-aaa1-98a507c295d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-40591" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}], \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Computing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)=0.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The vector $x$ can be written as $x={a}_{1}-{a}_{2}.$ So, $x$ lies in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ Therefore, the projection of $x$ onto $S$ is $x$ itself and the angle between $x$ and the subspace is equal to $0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-40591/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-40591"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-82979/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "fac9ef11-18d8-5518-88ca-6914d3e41fe4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82979/selection/adbad78d33491a071139fe589fefda7e9ba65cf97ebbcae14d252ac90459e198" :answer/id #uuid "997d7e10-f118-55ac-91f2-63df7be638f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82979/selection/e0bd991ac2e2f2a23ec920e03c6d5103be6b20c12a25d724ab290d4ba5292c3e" :answer/id #uuid "9ec2975e-fbdf-5ed0-85af-c9914aebcf94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82979/selection/82f0679237d603c9b88d77fbc6f240065a24a3716631fab4d9f58426a7f011e5" :answer/id #uuid "0b4b71f3-dc38-5620-9fce-87ea5ba4d627" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82979/selection/d62732e72e17b0393290bd9e548ef368f6b7424bded817d038b07db5bf5fcb98" :answer/id #uuid "b772fffd-95ff-5392-bd41-8a1fec375285" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82979/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9b1d76bc-0f00-5a24-82fb-37502bab4aa6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-82979/selection/82f0679237d603c9b88d77fbc6f240065a24a3716631fab4d9f58426a7f011e5" :answer-field/choices ["q-82979/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-82979/selection/adbad78d33491a071139fe589fefda7e9ba65cf97ebbcae14d252ac90459e198" "q-82979/selection/e0bd991ac2e2f2a23ec920e03c6d5103be6b20c12a25d724ab290d4ba5292c3e" "q-82979/selection/82f0679237d603c9b88d77fbc6f240065a24a3716631fab4d9f58426a7f011e5" "q-82979/selection/d62732e72e17b0393290bd9e548ef368f6b7424bded817d038b07db5bf5fcb98"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-82979" :question/id #uuid "3360a6d8-b9c1-5626-8aac-3aa15b49eddb" :question/math-academy-id "q-82979" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{36}\\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{6\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-82979/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93bf305-85ff-5455-91ba-ae60f3c1989d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-82979"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5573"] :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/assets/bc9289885d7d9db73bedf7486b311cf7333ea0cb72520ce3399a9b15f2c9b4c8.png)\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7173"] :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the plane $\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{v}+t\\mathbf{w},$ where $s,t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/assets/11a1a4da746f8286edde9e12f17a7eaae9f4a242dfdab8ed137674c2d8e0c04c.png) \nFind the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the plane given that $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{w}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5574"] :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/assets/7e767fa1b102062adfe30a12660ee0854ecd14829cd158160e95c9efbcc718b5.png)\n\nFind the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7309"] :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf v,\\mathbf w\\}$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068823/assets/665de193fe6fda3cfc5b5ec38f61f2b69ccb0e5d72514d6622b0815c5c3f474b.png)\nFind the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
